  "preview": "Two meshed gears have 36 and 60 teeth. A red dot is painted on one tooth of each. After how many rotations do the dots meet again? Prime factorization tells the story.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use prime factorization to find LCM and GCF in a concrete rotational context, and to reason about why the prime structure of two numbers controls how often patterns repeat.",
    "studentPrompt": "Two gears are meshed together so their teeth interlock. The smaller gear has 36 teeth. The larger gear has 60 teeth. Someone paints a red dot on one tooth of each gear, and lines the two dots up so they are touching at the start.\n\n1. Write 36 and 60 as products of prime factors. What primes do they share? What primes are unique to each?\n2. As the gears turn, every time one tooth of the small gear passes, one tooth of the big gear passes too. After how many teeth have passed will the two red dots meet again? Justify using your prime factorizations.\n3. How many full rotations has the small gear made? How many has the large gear made? Why are those numbers different?\n4. A third gear with 48 teeth is added to the system, meshed only with the small (36-tooth) gear. If a red dot is painted on the 48-tooth gear too, and all three dots start aligned, after how many teeth do ALL THREE dots realign?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "Two meshed metal gears: a smaller gear on the left with 36 teeth and a larger gear on the right with 60 teeth, each marked with a small red dot on one tooth.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><defs><radialGradient id=\"gearA\" cx=\"0.4\" cy=\"0.4\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#cbd5e1\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#64748b\"/></radialGradient><radialGradient id=\"gearB\" cx=\"0.4\" cy=\"0.4\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#e2e8f0\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#475569\"/></radialGradient></defs><rect width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#f8fafc\"/><g transform=\"translate(150,115)\"><g fill=\"url(#gearA)\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1\"><circle r=\"55\"/><g><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(30)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(60)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(90)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(120)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(150)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(180)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(210)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(240)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(270)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(300)\"/><rect x=\"-4\" y=\"-66\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" rx=\"1\" transform=\"rotate(330)\"/></g></g><circle r=\"10\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"-60\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/><text y=\"5\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">36</text></g><g transform=\"translate(335,115)\"><g fill=\"url(#gearB)\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1\"><circle r=\"80\"/><g><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(22.5)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(45)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(67.5)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(90)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(112.5)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(135)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(157.5)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(180)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(202.5)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(225)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(247.5)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(270)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(292.5)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(315)\"/><rect x=\"-5\" y=\"-92\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" rx=\"1.5\" transform=\"rotate(337.5)\"/></g></g><circle r=\"14\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><circle cx=\"-78\" cy=\"0\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/><text y=\"5\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">60</text></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Bring out two cardboard or plastic gears if you have them — or just spin your hands. Set the story: 'Two gears, meshed. Red dots line up. How many teeth pass before the dots line up AGAIN?' Resist the urge to mention LCM. Send them to the boards.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: physical gears, paper cutouts, or a gear simulation video",
      "Optional: factor tree templates"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "prime-factorization",
      "least-common-multiple",
      "greatest-common-factor",
      "factor-trees",
      "real-world-application"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. Low floor — every group can list multiples of 36 and 60 until they collide. The diamond is Q2's justification: groups that brute-force multiples get 180 but can't explain WHY 180. Groups that factor — 36 = 2^2 * 3^2 and 60 = 2^2 * 3 * 5 — see that the LCM is 2^2 * 3^2 * 5 = 180 and can defend it.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Listing multiples: 36, 72, 108, 144, 180... and 60, 120, 180. Match at 180.\n- Prime factor trees: 36 = 2 * 2 * 3 * 3, 60 = 2 * 2 * 3 * 5. LCM uses the MAX power of each prime: 2^2 * 3^2 * 5 = 180.\n- Some groups will compute (36 * 60) / GCF = 2160 / 12 = 180. Ask them why that works.\n- Q3: 180 / 36 = 5 small-gear rotations; 180 / 60 = 3 big-gear rotations. Small gear spins more because it's smaller.\n- Q4: 48 = 2^4 * 3. LCM(36, 60, 48) = 2^4 * 3^2 * 5 = 720 teeth.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating 36 * 60 = 2160 as the answer (correct only if 36 and 60 share no factors).\n- Adding 36 + 60 = 96 to find when they meet (this is not how meshing works).\n- For Q4, computing LCM in pairs without checking all three together (e.g., LCM(180, 48) is correct, but some take LCM(48, 36) = 144 and stop).\n- Forgetting that 'rotations of the small gear' is 180/36, not 180/60.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- One group listed multiples until they collided. Another factored. Why do both methods land on 180?\n- Why does the SMALLER gear make MORE rotations? Where does that show up in your factorization?\n- In Q4, the answer jumps from 180 to 720 when we add the 48-tooth gear. What does 48 contribute that 36 and 60 didn't already have?\n- If two gears have a PRIME number of teeth (say 17 and 23), what's the LCM? Why does that make engineers nervous?",
    "extensions": "- A clockmaker wants the hour, minute, and second hands of an analog clock to all align at 12 again. The gear ratios are driven by 12, 60, and 60. Use prime factorization to determine how many seconds it takes. (Answer: 12 hours = 43,200 seconds — every 12 hours, all three align.)\n- Two cicada species emerge every 13 and 17 years. Why are these prime numbers a survival advantage against a 4-year-cycle predator? Use prime factorization to argue when (or if) their cycles align.\n- Design a 3-gear system where the LCM of the tooth counts is exactly 360. List three valid combinations. What constraints does the prime factorization of 360 place on you?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. 36 = 2^2 * 3^2. 60 = 2^2 * 3 * 5. Shared primes: 2 (twice) and 3 (once). Unique: extra 3 in 36, the 5 in 60.\n2. The dots realign when the same number of teeth has passed on each gear AND that number is a multiple of both 36 and 60. The smallest such number is LCM(36, 60) = 2^2 * 3^2 * 5 = 180 teeth.\n3. Small gear: 180 / 36 = 5 rotations. Large gear: 180 / 60 = 3 rotations. The smaller gear spins more times because each rotation moves fewer teeth past the meshing point.\n4. 48 = 2^4 * 3. LCM(36, 60, 48) = 2^4 * 3^2 * 5 = 720 teeth. Small gear: 20 rotations. 60-tooth: 12 rotations. 48-tooth: 15 rotations.",
